# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /etc/pullfm/alert.env  -  root:root 0600, NEVER in git.
#
# This is the whole notification channel. Until this file exists on a node,
# every detector in this repository writes to a log that nobody reads.
#
# It is written by infra/observability/install-alert-env.sh, which resolves the
# values from 1Password at deploy time. This file is the SHAPE, with op://
# references where the values go. It contains no values and never will, because
# the repository is public and on ntfy the topic name IS the credential.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The full publish endpoint, including the topic. TREAT AS A SECRET.
#
# Anyone who has this URL can read every alert Pull.fm ever sends and publish
# forgeries into the same topic. That is a property of ntfy, not a mistake: a
# topic is a capability, and its only protection on a public instance is that
# nobody else knows the name. Which is why this is an op:// reference and why
# `install-alert-env.sh` never echoes the resolved value.
#
#   op://MCP/pull-fm/staging/ALERT_NTFY_URL
PULLFM_NTFY_URL=

# Optional bearer token. Empty for an anonymous public instance such as
# ntfy.sh; required for a self-hosted instance with auth enabled.
#
# If this is ever set to a token that also grants publish on topics belonging to
# something other than Pull.fm, read README.md section 2 first. That is the
# specific mistake this file is laid out to prevent.
#
#   op://MCP/pull-fm/staging/ALERT_NTFY_TOKEN
PULLFM_NTFY_TOKEN=

# Seconds before the SAME alert key may fire again. The watchdog runs every
# minute; without this a condition that stays true for a day is 1440
# notifications and the operator mutes the topic, which is the same coverage as
# never arming it.
PULLFM_ALERT_REPEAT_S=3600

# Appears in every alert title and body. Set it, or a staging page at 3am is
# indistinguishable from a production one.
PULLFM_ALERT_ENV_LABEL=staging
